    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> set out to keep human deeds from fading with time and to explain, among other things, why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and non-Greeks went to war with each other.</li><li>The work ranges over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Scythia and the Greek cities, and builds to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> and the Greek victories at Salamis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Mycale.</li><li>Its reliability has been disputed since antiquity, yet it remains the primary, often only, source for much of the Greek world and the Persian Empire in the two centuries before his time.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reported giant, fox-sized &quot;ants&quot; in an eastern province of the Persian Empire that unearthed gold dust. After journeys in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, Michel Peissel proposed that he may have meant the Himalayan marmot, whose burrows bring gold dust to the surface on the Deosai Plateau.</p></aside>
    </section>

