    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 430 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Nothing in the Histories can be dated with certainty later than this year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> is generally assumed to have died not long afterwards</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">425 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a>' comedy The Acharnians blames the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> on the abduction of some prostitutes, a mocking reference to the opening of the Histories</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early 2nd century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> 2099 preserves a fragment of Book VIII</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century</span><span class="rf-text">The oldest extant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the Histories, including the Codex Laurentianus A, date from the 9th and 10th centuries, in the Byzantine period</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1975</span><span class="rf-text">Gelonus, the Scythian city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> described, is rediscovered</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> of Halicarnassus lived from about 484 to about 425 BCE. He claimed to have travelled extensively around the ancient world, conducting interviews and collecting stories, and almost all of the territory the Histories covers lay within the Persian Empire. The opening lines state his purpose: to prevent the traces of human events from being erased by time, to preserve the fame of the important and remarkable achievements of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and non-Greeks, and to set out in particular the cause of the hostilities between them.</p><p>He had predecessors in prose. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a> began his Genealogies with the words "I write these things as they seem true to me; for the stories told by the Greeks are various and in my opinion absurd." Herodotus mentions Hecataeus twice, once mocking his naive genealogy and once quoting Athenian complaints about his handling of their national history, and it is possible that he borrowed much from him. Hecataeus, however, did not record events within living memory, and he did not set Greek oral traditions inside a wider history of the East. There is no proof that Herodotus took the scope of his own work, a grand theme of civilisations in conflict, from any predecessor.</p><p>Poets and story-tellers shaped him as well. Athenian tragedy gave him a world-view in which conflicting forces balance until the hubris of kings upsets them, and a model for episodic structure; passages echo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>'s Persae. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> was another source of inspiration, and Herodotus appears to have drawn on an Ionian tradition of story-telling in the way Homer drew on oral poetry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croesus.jpg" alt="Croesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croesus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Histories is one of the earliest accounts of the rise of the Persian Empire and of the events and causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> of the 5th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> portrays the conflict as one between the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and the forces of freedom, the Athenians and the confederacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> that united against the invaders. The nine books bear the names of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muses">Muses</a>. Book I (Clio) begins with the abductions of Io, Europa and Medea and moves through the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> and the oracle at Delphi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Book II (Euterpe) turns to the geography, customs and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Book III (Thalia) to Cambyses, the conspiracy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>, the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> and the twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>.</p><p>Book IV (Melpomene) is chiefly about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> and Darius's failed attack on them. Book V (Terpsichore) covers the revolt of Miletus and the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, and Book VI (Erato) the defeat of the Ionian fleet and the Athenian victory at Marathon under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>. Book VII (Polymnia) follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>' preparations, including his canal and bridges across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, and the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. In its summary, a Greek force of about 7,000 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonidas-i">Leonidas I</a> faces a Persian army that Herodotus puts at about 5,283,220. Book VIII (Urania) describes the Greek fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurybiades">Eurybiades</a>, the evacuation and destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and the battle of Salamis, and Book IX (Calliope) the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and the Greek victory at Mycale.</p><p>Each book also holds digressions, among them the tale of Arion carried to shore by a dolphin and an account of the Persian royal road from Sardis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/homer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/homer.jpg" alt="Homer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homer_At_the_British_Museum_2024_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net)., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Histories was at some point divided into nine books, the form found in modern editions. His preamble sets out his purpose as explanation: he discusses the reason or cause of events. This mode of explanation traces itself back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, who opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> with a question of causality, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> does when he asks why <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and barbarians went to war. His explanations cover a host of causes and emotions; it has been noted that the obligations of gratitude and revenge are for him fundamental human motives. Some readers hold that his habit of tying events to personal motives shows an inability to see broader reasons, while John Gould argues that he gave the rational reasons as his contemporaries understood them.</p><p>He attributes cause to both divine and human agents, not as exclusive alternatives but as interconnected. Geoffrey de Ste-Croix and Mabel Lang have argued that Fate is his ultimate understanding of causality, whereas Gould places him in a long line of story-tellers whose sense of what was "going to happen" belongs to narrative, not to a theory of historical necessity.</p><p>Herodotus uses, or claims to use, various sources. At times he sets out several accounts of one story, as with the Phoenician and Persian accounts of Io, and at times he arbitrates between them or claims authority, saying he heard something from the Delphians themselves. He probably knew no language but Greek and relied on translators within the multilingual Persian Empire. His reports on Greek events draw heavily on Periclean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and are often coloured by Athenian bias against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and Corinth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos.jpg" alt="Duris of Samos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos">Duris of Samos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_1333_Samian_decree_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The accuracy of the Histories has been controversial since his own era. In antiquity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> acquired a reputation as unreliable, biased and mendacious. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos">Duris of Samos</a> called him a "myth-monger", <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> said his works were full of legends or fables, the grammarian Harpocration wrote a book on "the lies of Herodotus", and Lucian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> denied him a place among the famous on the Island of the Blessed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Flavius Josephus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> also commented on the dispute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> was often preferred for truthfulness and reliability, even though he continued on foundations Herodotus had laid. Yet many, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, routinely cited Herodotus, and he was in general held in high esteem.</p><p>Charges of bias were made too. A work titled On the Malice of Herodotus, attributed to Plutarch or to a pseudonymous author, alleged prejudice against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, and a Corinthian Oration ascribed to Dio Chrysostom, or to another writer, alleged prejudice against Corinth. Plutarch called him philobarbaros, a pro-foreigner.</p><p>Some modern scholars still hold parts of the work unreliable. Detlev Fehling argues that Herodotus exaggerated the extent of his travels and invented his sources, and concludes that the work is intended as fiction; Boedeker agrees that much of it consists of literary devices. Alan B. Lloyd calls the work seriously defective as a historical document on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Others, among them Cheikh Anta Diop and Derek A. Welsby, defend his Egyptian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> material as careful and corroborated. Modern historians generally take a more positive view of its value as a source and have called him the father of comparative anthropology and of ethnography.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/troy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/troy.jpg" alt="Troy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walls_of_Troy_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CherryX, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The oldest extant copies of the Histories are Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the 9th and 10th centuries, notably the Codex Laurentianus A. Many early papyri also survive, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxyrhynchus">Oxyrhynchus</a> 18 and 19 and Papyrus Oxyrhynchus 2099, a fragment of Book VIII from the early 2nd century CE.</p><p>Several of the work's claims have been tested against later finds. Discoveries made since the end of the 19th century have generally added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s credibility. He described Gelonus, in Scythia, as a city thousands of times larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, which was widely disbelieved until it was rediscovered in 1975. The archaeological study of the now-submerged Egyptian city of Heracleion, and the recovery of the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a> stela, support his claim that Heracleion was founded during the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. A baris, a kind of boat the Histories describes, was found during excavation at the sunken port of Thonis-Heracleion.</p><p>Other passages remain disputed. Herodotus claimed to have visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> but does not mention the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanging-gardens-of-babylon">Hanging Gardens</a>, which has fed attacks on his credibility; Dalley has proposed that the Gardens stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> instead. His story of gold-digging ants was repeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> in the Naturalis Historia, and Michel Peissel suggested that the marmots of the Deosai Plateau lay behind it, perhaps through a confusion of the Persian words for marmot and mountain ant. Herodotus did not claim to have seen the creatures himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Histories is considered the founding work of history in Western literature. Although it is not an impartial record, it remains one of the West's most important sources and established the genre and study of history in the Western world. For the Greek world, the Persian Empire and the wider region in the two centuries before his time it has long served, and still serves, as the primary and often the only source. The Greek word historia, meaning research, gave the English word history.</p><p>Historians and thinkers have drawn on it in many ways. A. H. L. Heeren quoted it throughout his work, and writers on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, among them Constantin François de Chassebœuf, comte de Volney, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-e-b-du-bois">W. E. B. Du Bois</a>, valued its contribution to the history and ethnography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. Martin Bernal relied on it heavily in Black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> has been called the "father of history" and described as more modern than any other ancient historian in his approach to the ideal of total history.</p><p>Barry S. Strauss writes that Herodotus is one of the great storytellers and a philosopher with three themes: the struggle between East and West, the power of liberty, and the rise and fall of empires. John Herington described him as a centaur, a historian's human forepart joined to a body from an older and wilder realm of tale and myth. Historical novels also take material from the work.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Histories is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s Greek inquiry into the rise of the Persian Empire and the causes and course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>. Its reliability has been disputed since antiquity, yet it remains the primary source for much of its period and is regarded as the founding work of Western history.</em></p>

